  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dry weather will come to an end for many on Thursday \nas a cold front ushers in showers and thunderstorms. \n\nA ridge of high pressure supporting dry weather across the eastern \nGreat Lakes region this afternoon will move east tonight. GOES water \nvapor imagery shows a mid-level trough digging into the Upper Great \nLakes region with a progressive shortwave trough moving into the \nOhio Valley. Surface low pressure over Lake Superior with an \nassociated cold front will approach the region tonight. An \nincreasing southerly flow will keep conditions mild overnight. \n\nA pre-frontal trough is expected to move across Lake Erie late \ntonight into Thursday morning. Weak to moderate surface based \ninstability across the lake may aide in additional thunderstorm \ndevelopment and result in showers and thunderstorms across far \nwestern NY in the early morning hours. As the trough moves east and \naway from higher instability, coverage of showers and thunderstorms \nis expected to diminish through mid-morning. Then, daytime heating \nand dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s will generate weak to moderate \nsurface based instability across the region Thursday afternoon. \nAdditional scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop along and ahead of multiple frontal boundaries moving across \nthe region. 0-6 km bulk shear of 20-25 knots and PWATS of 1.4\" (75th \npercentile) will support a few strong to severe thunderstorms from \nthe Finger Lakes region to the southern Tug Hill region. \n\nAs most of the activity shifts east of the forecast area late \nThursday afternoon, showers and a few thunderstorms may linger along \na convergence zone across the Niagara Frontier into the Finger Lakes \nregion. This zone of showers is expected to extend into the Finger \nLakes region to southern Tug Hill region into Thursday evening. A \ncold front will approach the forecast area Thursday night. There is \na low chance of additional light showers overnight. \n\nRainfall amounts will be widely scattered with many locations \nseeing less than a quarter inch through Thursday night. The \nheaviest rainfall will be confined to thunderstorms across the \nFinger Lakes region to the southern Tug Hill region. \n\nKey Message 2...Dry weather will return Friday and last through the \nweekend.\n\nA broad mid level trough will advance east across Quebec Friday. A \ncooler airmass rounding into the base of the trough may support a \nfew scattered lake effect rain showers east of Lake Ontario in the \nmorning, but these will end by afternoon as surface high pressure \nbuilds into the eastern Great Lakes with lowering inversion heights \nand a drying airmass.\n\nThe surface high will linger across the eastern Great Lakes and New \nEngland Saturday, then drift off the east coast Sunday. This surface \nhigh and building heights aloft will support mainly dry weather \nthrough the weekend. An initially cool airmass Friday and Friday \nnight will give way to a day to day warming trend, with above \naverage temperatures returning Sunday into early next week.",
